{"module":"wealth","voice":"female","duration":272.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Wealth: built, blocked and taken","start":0.0,"end":2.45,"words":[[0.05,0.42],[0.68,1.04],[1.3,1.67],[1.67,1.89],[1.89,2.33]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Income is what you earn; wealth is what you own and pass down.","start":3.25,"end":7.19,"words":[[3.31,3.7],[3.7,3.86],[3.86,4.09],[4.09,4.25],[4.25,4.48],[4.75,5.06],[5.06,5.22],[5.22,5.45],[5.45,5.61],[5.61,5.84],[5.84,6.08],[6.08,6.31],[6.31,6.7]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Wealth pays for college, starts businesses, survives emergencies and posts bail.","start":7.54,"end":14.37,"words":[[7.59,7.95],[7.95,8.31],[8.31,8.49],[8.49,9.12],[9.43,9.88],[9.88,10.69],[11.01,11.64],[11.64,12.54],[12.54,12.81],[12.81,13.35],[13.35,13.8]]},{"el":"intro","text":"The racial wealth gap isn't mainly about what people earn today.","start":14.72,"end":18.28,"words":[[14.77,14.9],[14.9,15.28],[15.28,15.54],[15.54,15.73],[15.73,16.05],[16.05,16.43],[16.43,16.75],[16.75,16.95],[16.95,17.27],[17.27,17.39],[17.39,17.78]]},{"el":"intro","text":"It's the inheritance of policy.","start":18.63,"end":20.8,"words":[[18.69,18.88],[18.88,19.02],[19.02,19.68],[19.68,19.81],[19.81,20.27]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The gap","start":21.15,"end":21.94,"words":[[21.19,21.35],[21.35,21.6]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the Federal Reserve's 2022 Survey of Consumer Finances, the median white household had about $285,000 in wealth, the median Black household about $45,000, and the median Hispanic household about $62,000.","start":22.64,"end":39.78,"words":[[22.7,22.84],[22.84,22.98],[22.98,23.48],[23.48,23.91],[23.91,24.9],[24.9,25.26],[25.26,25.4],[25.4,25.9],[25.9,26.61],[26.86,27.0],[27.0,27.5],[27.5,27.78],[27.78,28.43],[28.43,28.64],[28.64,28.99],[28.99,31.13],[31.13,31.27],[31.27,31.63],[31.88,32.02],[32.02,32.52],[32.52,32.8],[32.8,33.44],[33.44,33.8],[33.8,35.22],[35.47,35.68],[35.68,35.83],[35.83,36.32],[36.32,36.89],[36.89,37.53],[37.53,37.89],[37.89,39.31]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Wealth gaps are far larger than income gaps because wealth builds over generations, and so does its absence.","start":40.13,"end":47.08,"words":[[40.17,40.49],[40.49,40.8],[40.8,40.87],[40.87,41.03],[41.03,41.42],[41.42,41.65],[41.65,42.04],[42.04,42.35],[42.35,42.74],[42.74,43.05],[43.05,43.44],[43.44,43.75],[43.75,44.75],[45.03,45.26],[45.26,45.49],[45.49,45.73],[45.73,45.96],[45.96,46.5]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Forty acres, promised and taken back","start":47.98,"end":50.78,"words":[[47.98,48.31],[48.31,48.81],[49.1,49.68],[49.68,49.93],[49.93,50.42],[50.42,50.67]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In January 1865, General Sherman's Special Field Order No. 15 set aside coastal land from South Carolina to Florida for freed families, in plots of up to 40 acres.","start":51.48,"end":64.1,"words":[[51.53,51.68],[51.68,52.37],[52.37,53.59],[53.86,54.47],[54.47,54.93],[54.93,55.39],[55.39,55.69],[55.69,55.92],[55.92,56.23],[56.61,57.07],[57.07,57.3],[57.3,57.68],[57.68,58.22],[58.22,58.52],[58.52,58.83],[58.83,59.13],[59.13,59.82],[59.82,59.97],[59.97,60.51],[60.51,60.66],[60.66,60.97],[60.97,61.58],[61.85,62.0],[62.0,62.38],[62.38,62.53],[62.53,62.69],[62.69,62.84],[62.84,63.15],[63.15,63.6]]},{"el":"s1","text":"That fall, President Andrew Johnson ordered most of it returned to former Confederates.","start":64.45,"end":69.97,"words":[[64.49,64.7],[64.7,64.99],[65.24,65.89],[65.89,66.25],[66.25,66.76],[66.76,67.05],[67.05,67.41],[67.41,67.55],[67.55,67.7],[67.7,68.13],[68.13,68.28],[68.28,68.57],[68.57,69.43]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Freedman's Savings Bank, chartered by Congress in 1865, collected the savings of more than 60,000 Black depositors.","start":70.32,"end":79.77,"words":[[70.37,70.53],[70.53,71.15],[71.15,71.69],[71.69,72.08],[72.35,72.82],[72.82,73.05],[73.05,73.59],[73.59,73.75],[73.75,74.99],[75.26,75.88],[75.88,76.04],[76.04,76.58],[76.58,76.74],[76.74,76.89],[76.89,77.13],[77.13,78.14],[78.14,78.45],[78.45,79.22]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Mismanaged by its white trustees, it collapsed in 1874, and many depositors lost most of their money.","start":80.12,"end":87.69,"words":[[80.18,80.92],[80.92,81.14],[81.14,81.36],[81.36,81.65],[81.65,82.24],[82.5,82.64],[82.64,83.16],[83.16,83.31],[83.31,84.41],[84.67,84.89],[84.89,85.18],[85.18,85.84],[85.84,86.14],[86.14,86.5],[86.5,86.65],[86.65,86.87],[86.87,87.24]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The New Deal left most Black workers out","start":88.59,"end":91.38,"words":[[88.65,88.83],[88.83,89.1],[89.1,89.37],[89.37,89.73],[89.73,90.18],[90.18,90.55],[90.55,91.0],[91.0,91.27]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Documented: the Social Security Act (1935) and the Fair Labor Standards Act (1938) excluded agricultural and domestic workers, the jobs most Black workers in the South held at the time.","start":92.08,"end":105.95,"words":[[92.14,93.02],[93.27,93.42],[93.42,93.86],[93.86,94.59],[94.59,94.81],[94.81,95.91],[96.17,96.39],[96.39,96.53],[96.53,96.75],[96.75,97.12],[97.12,97.71],[97.71,97.93],[97.93,99.02],[99.28,99.94],[99.94,100.89],[100.89,101.11],[101.11,101.7],[101.7,102.14],[102.39,102.54],[102.54,102.91],[102.91,103.27],[103.27,103.57],[103.57,103.93],[103.93,104.08],[104.08,104.23],[104.23,104.52],[104.52,104.81],[104.81,104.96],[104.96,105.1],[105.1,105.47]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Most Black workers were left out of old-age insurance and minimum-wage protection for years; the exclusions were narrowed in the 1950s.","start":106.3,"end":116.37,"words":[[106.35,106.77],[106.77,107.1],[107.1,107.51],[107.51,107.68],[107.68,108.01],[108.01,108.25],[108.25,108.42],[108.42,109.08],[109.08,109.82],[109.82,110.07],[110.07,111.06],[111.06,111.81],[111.81,111.97],[111.97,112.39],[112.68,112.84],[112.84,113.67],[113.67,113.83],[113.83,114.33],[114.33,114.49],[114.49,114.66],[114.66,115.82]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Debated: why.","start":116.72,"end":117.87,"words":[[116.77,117.19],[117.35,117.54]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Historian Ira Katznelson and others argue Southern Democrats in Congress insisted on the exclusions to protect the racial order.","start":118.22,"end":125.73,"words":[[118.27,118.89],[118.89,119.17],[119.17,119.87],[119.87,120.08],[120.08,120.35],[120.35,120.63],[120.63,120.98],[120.98,121.61],[121.61,121.75],[121.75,122.23],[122.23,122.79],[122.79,122.93],[122.93,123.07],[123.07,123.76],[123.76,123.9],[123.9,124.39],[124.39,124.53],[124.53,124.95],[124.95,125.22]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Other scholars argue administrative difficulty was the main reason.","start":126.08,"end":129.92,"words":[[126.13,126.33],[126.33,126.73],[126.73,127.0],[127.0,127.87],[127.87,128.48],[128.48,128.68],[128.68,128.81],[128.81,129.08],[129.08,129.49]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The effect on Black families is not in dispute.","start":130.27,"end":133.12,"words":[[130.32,130.45],[130.45,130.79],[130.79,130.92],[130.92,131.18],[131.18,131.65],[131.65,131.78],[131.78,131.98],[131.98,132.11],[132.11,132.64]]},{"el":"h3","text":"The GI Bill and the suburbs","start":134.02,"end":135.86,"words":[[134.08,134.23],[134.23,134.69],[134.69,134.92],[134.92,135.15],[135.15,135.3],[135.3,135.76]]},{"el":"s3","text":"After World War II, the GI Bill paid for college and guaranteed home loans for 16 million veterans.","start":136.56,"end":143.8,"words":[[136.61,136.91],[136.91,137.21],[137.21,137.36],[137.36,138.03],[138.29,138.44],[138.44,138.89],[138.89,139.11],[139.11,139.41],[139.41,139.56],[139.56,140.0],[140.0,140.23],[140.23,140.82],[140.82,141.12],[141.12,141.5],[141.5,141.64],[141.64,142.17],[142.17,142.61],[142.61,143.28]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Benefits were run by local officials and banks, who largely shut Black veterans out.","start":144.15,"end":149.71,"words":[[144.21,144.79],[144.79,144.93],[144.93,145.15],[145.15,145.37],[145.37,145.8],[145.8,146.31],[146.31,146.53],[146.53,146.97],[147.22,147.36],[147.36,147.8],[147.8,148.02],[148.02,148.31],[148.31,148.89],[148.89,149.18]]},{"el":"s3","text":"A 1947 survey of 13 Mississippi cities found that of 3,229 VA-guaranteed home loans, two went to Black veterans.","start":150.06,"end":160.36,"words":[[150.11,150.25],[150.25,151.43],[151.43,151.8],[151.8,151.95],[151.95,152.31],[152.31,152.9],[152.9,153.27],[153.27,153.64],[153.64,153.86],[153.86,154.0],[154.0,156.28],[156.28,157.24],[157.24,157.53],[157.53,157.97],[158.23,158.38],[158.38,158.67],[158.67,158.82],[158.82,159.11],[159.11,159.78]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Federal Housing Administration's underwriting manual recommended racial covenants, and new suburbs such as Levittown refused to sell to Black families.","start":160.71,"end":170.1,"words":[[160.78,160.92],[160.92,161.4],[161.4,161.8],[161.8,162.82],[162.82,163.5],[163.5,163.97],[163.97,164.65],[164.65,165.06],[165.06,165.73],[165.97,166.18],[166.18,166.38],[166.38,166.79],[166.79,167.06],[167.06,167.19],[167.19,167.8],[167.8,168.28],[168.28,168.41],[168.41,168.62],[168.62,168.75],[168.75,169.02],[169.02,169.57]]},{"el":"s3","text":"White families built wealth through rising home values; most Black families were shut out of the biggest wealth-building program in U.S. history.","start":170.45,"end":179.71,"words":[[170.5,170.8],[170.8,171.34],[171.34,171.64],[171.64,171.94],[171.94,172.17],[172.17,172.63],[172.63,172.93],[172.93,173.47],[173.73,174.11],[174.11,174.42],[174.42,174.95],[174.95,175.1],[175.1,175.33],[175.33,175.56],[175.56,175.71],[175.71,175.87],[175.87,176.32],[176.32,177.08],[177.08,177.69],[177.69,177.85],[177.85,178.23],[178.61,179.22]]},{"el":"h4","text":"From redlining to subprime","start":180.61,"end":182.37,"words":[[180.61,180.9],[180.9,181.53],[181.53,181.67],[181.67,182.24]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Redlined neighborhoods were starved of ordinary loans for decades (see the module on place).","start":183.07,"end":188.02,"words":[[183.12,183.65],[183.65,184.26],[184.26,184.39],[184.39,184.73],[184.73,184.86],[184.86,185.33],[185.33,185.67],[185.67,185.8],[185.8,186.27],[186.27,186.4],[186.4,186.54],[186.54,186.94],[186.94,187.08],[187.08,187.48]]},{"el":"s4","text":"When credit finally came, much of it was predatory.","start":188.37,"end":191.76,"words":[[188.42,188.62],[188.62,189.0],[189.0,189.45],[189.45,189.77],[190.0,190.25],[190.25,190.38],[190.38,190.51],[190.51,190.7],[190.7,191.34]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Before 2008, Black and Latino borrowers were steered into subprime loans even when they qualified for better ones; Countrywide (2011, $335 million) and Wells Fargo (2012, $175 million) settled federal charges over it.","start":192.11,"end":211.15,"words":[[192.16,192.46],[192.46,193.41],[193.67,193.96],[193.96,194.18],[194.18,194.7],[194.7,195.21],[195.21,195.36],[195.36,195.73],[195.73,196.02],[196.02,196.61],[196.61,196.98],[196.98,197.27],[197.27,197.49],[197.49,197.71],[197.71,198.37],[198.37,198.52],[198.52,198.82],[198.82,199.18],[199.44,200.17],[200.17,201.2],[201.46,203.23],[203.23,203.67],[203.92,204.14],[204.14,204.44],[204.44,204.81],[204.81,205.69],[205.95,207.93],[207.93,208.37],[208.63,209.07],[209.07,209.58],[209.58,210.1],[210.1,210.39],[210.39,210.61]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Black and Latino families lost a larger share of their wealth in the foreclosure crisis.","start":211.5,"end":217.4,"words":[[211.57,211.9],[211.9,212.15],[212.15,212.72],[212.72,213.29],[213.29,213.62],[213.62,213.79],[213.79,214.2],[214.2,214.44],[214.44,214.61],[214.61,214.85],[214.85,215.18],[215.18,215.34],[215.34,215.51],[215.51,216.16],[216.16,216.82]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The gap continues through appraisals: a 2018 Brookings study found that homes in majority-Black neighborhoods are valued about $48,000 less than similar homes in neighborhoods with few Black residents.","start":217.75,"end":231.85,"words":[[217.8,217.96],[217.96,218.19],[218.19,218.9],[218.9,219.13],[219.13,219.91],[220.19,220.34],[220.34,221.28],[221.28,221.83],[221.83,222.22],[222.22,222.61],[222.61,222.85],[222.85,223.24],[223.24,223.4],[223.4,224.41],[224.41,225.12],[225.12,225.2],[225.2,225.66],[225.66,226.06],[226.06,227.54],[227.54,227.78],[227.78,228.01],[228.01,228.48],[228.48,228.87],[228.87,229.03],[229.03,229.73],[229.73,229.97],[229.97,230.2],[230.2,230.52],[230.52,231.3]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Rebuilding","start":232.75,"end":233.64,"words":[[232.8,233.54]]},{"el":"s5","text":"What builds wealth is ownership: homes, businesses, land and savings that compound.","start":234.34,"end":239.49,"words":[[234.39,234.58],[234.58,234.9],[234.9,235.15],[235.15,235.28],[235.28,235.78],[236.01,236.39],[236.61,237.18],[237.4,237.66],[237.66,237.85],[237.85,238.29],[238.29,238.48],[238.48,239.05]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Tools with a record include down-payment assistance, Black-owned banks and community development financial institutions (CDFIs), community land trusts, help resolving “heirs' property” so families keep their land, and “baby bonds” (Connecticut created the first state program in 2021).","start":239.84,"end":259.41,"words":[[239.89,240.2],[240.2,240.43],[240.43,240.58],[240.58,240.96],[240.96,241.42],[241.42,242.25],[242.25,242.94],[243.2,243.81],[243.81,244.19],[244.19,244.42],[244.42,245.11],[245.11,245.94],[245.94,246.63],[246.63,247.54],[247.54,248.15],[248.42,249.1],[249.1,249.41],[249.41,249.94],[250.21,250.51],[250.51,251.12],[251.12,251.35],[251.35,251.88],[251.88,252.11],[252.11,252.64],[252.64,252.87],[252.87,253.1],[253.1,253.48],[253.74,253.97],[253.97,254.35],[254.35,254.73],[254.73,255.42],[255.42,256.1],[256.1,256.26],[256.26,256.56],[256.56,256.94],[256.94,257.55],[257.55,257.7],[257.7,258.92]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Evanston, Illinois, began paying housing reparations in 2021.","start":259.76,"end":264.96,"words":[[259.81,260.42],[260.66,261.14],[261.38,261.72],[261.72,262.07],[262.07,262.48],[262.48,263.23],[263.23,263.37],[263.37,264.46]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The Learn quests include ways people who have built wealth can help others build it, beyond donating.","start":265.31,"end":271.44,"words":[[265.35,265.5],[265.5,265.72],[265.72,266.16],[266.16,266.6],[266.6,266.9],[266.9,267.26],[267.26,267.41],[267.41,267.63],[267.63,267.93],[267.93,268.22],[268.22,268.44],[268.44,268.74],[268.74,269.03],[269.03,269.33],[269.33,269.55],[269.81,270.25],[270.25,270.99]]}]}